-- title:
--   Tate-curve point of order M' cut out by kernel polynomials
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ and $\Lambda$ be fields with decidable equality, $\Lambda$ an algebra over the Laurent series field $F((\mathsf q))$, let $q,\ell$ be primes and $M'$ a nonzero natural number with $q\nmid M'$, $\ell\nmid M'$ and $M'\neq 0$ in $F$, let $\zeta\in F$ be a primitive $M'$-th root of unity, and let $C$ be a Weierstrass variable change over $\Lambda$. Write $E$ for the base change to $\Lambda$ of [`ModularCurve.tateBase F (q * ℓ)`](def/ModularCurve_TateSlots.html#L46), the universal Tate curve over $F((\mathsf q))$ pushed forward along the exponent-multiplication homomorphism `qExpand F (q * ℓ)`, and $E^{C}=C\bullet E$ for its transform by $C$. The assertion is that there is an affine point $g$ on $E^{C}$ with the following four properties. First, $n\cdot g=0$ if and only if $M'\mid n$. Second, for every $n$ with $M'\nmid n$ the pair of coordinates obtained by applying `algebraMap (LaurentSeries F) Λ` to the two components of the toric point [`ModularCurve.toricPoint F (q * ℓ) (ζ ^ n)`](def/ModularCurve_TateSlots.html#L125) is nonsingular on $E$, and transporting $n\cdot g$ back along $C$ via [`WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.vcFun`](def/WeierstrassCurve_VariableChangePointEquiv.html#L114) yields exactly the affine point with those coordinates. Third, the additive order of $g$ is $M'$, and for each prime $p\mid M'$, putting $k=v_p(M')$ and $\zeta_p=\zeta^{M'/p^{k}}$, whenever some multiple $n\cdot g$ is an affine point with coordinates $(x_1,y_1)$ and has additive order $p^{k}$, then $x_1$ is a root of the polynomial `kernelVariableChangeDeg C (gamma0PowDeg p k) h_p`, where $h_p=\prod_{1\le a\le p^{k}/2,\ p\nmid a}\bigl(X-\text{(first toric coordinate of }\zeta_p^{a}\text{, pushed to }\Lambda)\bigr)$, `gamma0PowDeg p k` is $1$ if $p^{k}=2$ and $\varphi(p^{k})/2$ otherwise, and `kernelVariableChangeDeg C d h` denotes $u^{-2d}\,h(u^{2}X+r)$ for the unit $u$ and translation $r$ of $C$. Fourth, conversely, for each prime $p\mid M'$ an element $x_1\in\Lambda$ is a root of that same polynomial if and only if there is a point $P$ of $E^{C}$ lying in the subgroup of integer multiples of $g$, of additive order $p^{k}$, which is affine with first coordinate $x_1$.
--
--   This packages the classical Tate-curve computation of torsion: the $M'$-torsion point whose multiples are the toric points $\zeta^n$, together with the statement that the $p$-primary kernel polynomials (transformed by the variable change $C$) have as roots exactly the $x$-coordinates of the points of order $p^{v_p(M')}$ in the cyclic group generated by it. It supplies the torsion point and the root-set description used when comparing $j$-invariants of cyclic quotients at the Tate parameter, in [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.algebraMap_jqNModC_eq_cyclicQuotientJ_of_eq_map_tatePoint_gamma0Pow`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.algebraMap_jqNModC_eq_cyclicQuotientJ_of_eq_map_tatePoint_gamma0Pow).
